Cuaderno_2013_1_M	60	answer	4	Mycobacterium avium-intracellulare.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	61	question	-	An 82-year-old patient comes to the clinic due to sudden and frequent dizziness, without prodromes, of short duration and that subside spontaneously, without ever presenting syncope. The physical examination and the baseline electrocardiogram are normal. In an ambulatory record of the electrocardiogram during 24 hours, short duration phases of absence of P waves before the QRS are detected with an escape rhythm of the atrio-ventricular junction with narrow QRS at 40 bpm and a wave at the beginning of the ST segment corresponding to a retrograde P. No periods of asystole greater than 3 seconds are detected. Before this you would say:
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	61	answer	0	The patient has an atrioventricular block of 3rd degree and requires the implantation of a pacemaker.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	61	answer	1	The absence of periods of asystole> 3 seconds excludes a cardiac cause of dizziness.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	61	answer	2	A pharmacological treatment that would increase conduction in the atrio-ventricular node would be indicated.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	61	answer	3	The patient presents a sinus dysfunction with sinoatrial block and requires implantation of a pacemaker due to the presence of symptoms.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	61	answer	4	The alterations detected in the ambulatory electrocardiogram are typical of patients of this age and there is no indication of therapeutic intervention.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	62	question	-	A 82-year-old hypertensive woman on treatment with atenolol, hydrochlorothiazide and digoxin. He goes to the emergency room for atrial fibrillation and is administered verapamil i.v. Complete atrioventricular block is evidenced in ECG. What is the most likely cause of this clinical situation?
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	62	answer	0	Digitalis intoxication by pharmacokinetic interaction by verapamil.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	62	answer	1	Hypokalemia due to the administration of thiazide and digoxin.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	62	answer	2	Pharmacodynamic interaction of beta-blocker, digoxin and verapamil.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	62	answer	3	Hypotensive effect of the thiazide diuretic.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	62	answer	4	Cardiac arrhythmia due to verapamil.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	63	question	-	A 45-year-old woman with a history of a heart murmur detected in the pediatric age. Enter the Emergency Department for symptoms of palpitations, easy fatigue and malignant edema. Physical examination reveals absence of cyanosis. Oxygen Saturation by pulse oximeter 97%. TA: 120/80 mm Hg. Irregular heart rate at 100 bpm. Systolic ejection murmur (2/6) in pulmonary focus. 2nd R unfolded, wide and fixed. No death rattles. Slight hepatomegaly (2-3 cm). Slight malleolar edema. ECG: complete arrhythmia due to atrial fibrillation at 100 bpm. AQRS: + 120º. Conduction disorder of the right branch of the bundle of His. What is your diagnostic orientation?
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	63	answer	0	Ventricular communication.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	63	answer	1	Aortic stenosis.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	63	answer	2	Mitral stenosis.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	63	answer	3	Interauricular communication.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	63	answer	4	Persistent ductus arteriosus.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	64	question	-	Before a 60-year-old diabetic patient with stable angina secondary to ischemic heart disease due to subtotal obstruction in the middle third of the anterior descending coronary artery, what treatment would you propose?
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	64	answer	0	Doctor with vasodilators and beta-blockers to prevent angina.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	64	answer	1	Expectant with rigorous rest since the decrease in myocardial oxygen demand should decrease angina.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	64	answer	2	Surgical to revascularize the ischemic myocardium by means of a by-pass of the left mammary artery distal to the lesion in the diseased coronary artery.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	64	answer	3	Dilation of the coronary artery lesion by means of a therapeutic catheterization.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	64	answer	4	Therapeutic catheterization to dilate the injury of the diseased artery and implantation of a "stent" in the dilated area.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	65	question	-	In which patient would an urgent coronary angiography be performed?
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	65	answer	0	66-year-old diabetic woman with a single episode of pain at rest and elevation of troponins.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	65	answer	1	A 77-year-old man admitted for two 30-minute episodes of pain with a decrease in the ST segment on the ECG during the episode of pain.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	65	answer	2	A 55-year-old man who, after a week of treatment with double antiaggregation, underwent an isotopic ergometry showing extensive ischemia in the anterior face.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	65	answer	3	A 65-year-old woman with pain at rest and positive clinical ergometry in the third stage of Bruce.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	65	answer	4	A 55-year-old patient admitted for chest pain and dyspnea saturating 80% despite the administration of oxygen and with an ECG with ST-segment depression in the anterolateral aspect that does not revert with the administration of intravenous nitroglycerin.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	66	question	-	What electrocardiographic alteration is interpreted as a subendocardial lesion?
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	66	answer	0	The reversal of the T wave.
