Cuaderno_2013_1_M	72	answer	3	It is present in patients with severe mitral regurgitation.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	72	answer	4	It can appear in normal children and in patients with high cardiac output.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	73	question	-	In choosing the type of cardiac valvular prosthesis (biological or mechanical) to be implanted in a patient, various aspects and characteristics of the patient and the prosthesis must be considered. In view of the aforementioned, point out the INCORRECT statement:
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	73	answer	0	Permanent anticoagulation is necessary in mechanical prostheses.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	73	answer	1	In general, biological prostheses are indicated in young patients, with long life expectancy.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	73	answer	2	Biological prostheses would be indicated in cases that present a formal contraindication for anticoagulation.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	73	answer	3	The rate of structural deterioration of a biological prosthesis is inversely proportional to the age of the subject.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	73	answer	4	Biological prostheses do not require permanent anticoagulation.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	74	question	-	Acute ischemia of a limb consists of a sharp decrease in arterial perfusion. It is important to differentiate between thrombosis and arterial embolism to be able to indicate the most appropriate treatment. Which of the following is correct?
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	74	answer	0	The presence of embolic focus and distal pulses in the contralateral limb leads to the suspicion of arterial thrombosis.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	74	answer	1	If the patient has a history of claudication and the clinic is of sudden onset, we will suspect arterial embolism in the first place.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	74	answer	2	If the contralateral limb has all the pulses present and the clinic starts slowly, we will suspect an arterial thrombosis.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	74	answer	3	If the clinic starts abruptly in a patient with embolism focus, we will suspect arterial embolism.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	74	answer	4	If the patient is a carrier of a previous arterial derivation, we will suspect arterial embolism.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	75	question	-	The characteristic Rovsing sign in acute appendicitis consists of:
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	75	answer	0	Pain in the epigastric pressure when applying a firm and persistent pressure on McBurney's point.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	75	answer	1	Acute pain that appears when the appendix is ​​compressed between the abdominal wall and the iliac crest.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	75	answer	2	Passive rebound sensitivity in the abdominal wall.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	75	answer	3	Loss of abdominal sensation when contracting the muscles of the abdominal wall.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	75	answer	4	Pain at McBurney's point when compressing the lower left quadrant of the abdomen.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	76	question	-	Regarding the Gastrointestinal Stromal Tumors (GIST), it is true that:
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	76	answer	0	These are tumors of epithelial origin, located mainly at the level of the mucosa of the digestive tract.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	76	answer	1	The mutation of the c-kit gene, which codes for a tyrosine kinase receptor, is characteristic of these tumors.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	76	answer	2	The treatment of localized tumors smaller than 2 cm is fundamentally medical, with the use of Imatinib.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	76	answer	3	They are tumors that only exceptionally bleed.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	76	answer	4	The most frequent localization of these tumors is the small intestine.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	77	question	-	A 45-year-old man underwent a truncal vagotomy and antrectomy with Billroth II reconstruction, for suffering from a chronic peptic ulcer disease with pylorus-duodenal stenosis. Six weeks after the surgery, he reported that shortly after (less than half an hour) of the ingestions he presented with a nauseating state, asthenia and sweating, dizziness and abdominal cramps, usually accompanied by diarrheal descent. Which of the following is the most appropriate attitude for its initial management?
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	77	answer	0	Apply treatment with a somatostatin analog (octeotide).
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	77	answer	1	Follow specific dietary measures.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	77	answer	2	Perform a test treatment with a benzodiazepine.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	77	answer	3	Perform the search for a probable neuroendocrine tumor (eg, carcinoid).
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	77	answer	4	Indicate surgical treatment to perform an anti-peristaltic gastro-jejunostomy in Roux-en-Y.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	78	question	-	Within the congenital anomalies of the abdominal wall are the omphalocele and gastroschisis. Both entities are defects of the wall. What is the difference between them?
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	78	answer	0	Omphalocele occurs at the umbilical level and gastroschisis at the epigastric level.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	78	answer	1	The defect, in the case of gastroschisis, is larger than the omphalocele.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	78	answer	2	In the omphalocele, a peritoneal sac covers the abdominal contents and not gastroschisis.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	78	answer	3	Omphalocele, unlike gastroschisis, is frequently associated with intestinal atresia.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	78	answer	4	Unlike the omphalocele, in gastroschisis the surgical treatment can be deferred.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	79	question	-	A 78-year-old woman went to the Emergency Department for pain in the left iliac fossa of 24 hours of evolution associated with fever and occasional vomiting. On examination, pain is palpated selectively in the left iliac fossa with a feeling of occupation, defense and positive decompression. Upon suspicion of acute diverticulitis. Which of the following statements is correct?
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	79	answer	0	The safest and best performing complementary scan is the enema with barium contrast.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	79	answer	1	In the case of a pelvic abscess, the placement of a percutaneous drainage guided with CT or ultrasound is indicated.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	79	answer	2	If surgical intervention is necessary after the acute episode is resolved, the laparoscopic approach is contraindicated.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	79	answer	3	In the case of acute uncomplicated diverticulitis, elective sigmoidectomy is indicated after the first acute episode has been cured.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	79	answer	4	If generalized peritonitis occurs, the most appropriate surgical technique is the practice of a derivative colostomy without resection of the affected sigmoid segment.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	80	question	-	Regarding intestinal polyposis, which of the following syndromes is NOT hereditary?
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	80	answer	0	Gardner syndrome.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	80	answer	1	Cronkhite-Canada syndrome.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	80	answer	2	Turcot syndrome.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	80	answer	3	Juvenile polyposis
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	80	answer	4	Peutz-Jeghers syndrome.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	81	question	-	A 55-year-old man, the father of a celiac son, with iron deficiency anemia and a recent increase in the rhythm of deposition, has been determined to have a negative HLA-DQ2 and HLADQ8 allele. Which diagnostic study is the most appropriate in this case?
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	81	answer	0	Determination of IgA anti-transglutaminase antibodies.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	81	answer	1	Upper digestive endoscopy with taking of duodenal biopsies.
