Cuaderno_2013_1_M	190	question	-	In a clinical study to evaluate the efficacy of a new medication in the treatment of migraine crisis (point out the CORRECT):
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	190	answer	0	Strict inclusion criteria increase the external validity of the study.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	190	answer	1	Strict exclusion criteria reduce the internal validity of the study.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	190	answer	2	Control with a placebo arm increases the external validity of the study.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	190	answer	3	Masking reduces the internal validity of the study.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	190	answer	4	Randomization increases the internal validity of the study.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	191	question	-	We want to know if coffee consumption can be associated with neonatal malformation, so a control case study is designed. We interview a group of women who have had children with malformations and the same in a group of mothers without children with malformations. The interviews will be conducted by two interviewers trained through a previously validated questionnaire. In addition, interviewers ignore whether the interviewee is a case or a control. What kind of bias can we introduce?
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	191	answer	0	Information bias
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	191	answer	1	Memory bias
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	191	answer	2	Interviewer bias
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	191	answer	3	Selection bias
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	191	answer	4	Ecological fallacy
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	192	question	-	We want to study if Vitamin D can increase the incidence of breast cancer. In our area we have a population registry of cancer. To achieve our goal, all women diagnosed with breast cancer are selected from the register we have and each case pairs with two controls. What study design has been chosen?
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	192	answer	0	Prospective cohort study.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	192	answer	1	Cases and controls study.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	192	answer	2	Case-control study nested.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	192	answer	3	Retrospective cohort study.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	192	answer	4	Ecological study
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	193	question	-	We want to conduct a study to assess the effect of exposure to alcohol consumption on pancreatic cancer. It is possible that the relationship may be affected by the effect of exposure to tobacco use. If you only want to analyze the effect of alcohol consumption, what type of bias can occur?
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	193	answer	0	Exposure bias
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	193	answer	1	Diagnostic bias
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	193	answer	2	Performance bias
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	193	answer	3	Bias due to surveillance effect (or Hawthorne).
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	193	answer	4	Confusing bias
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	194	question	-	A study is conducted to evaluate the relationship between lung cancer and exposure to silica. 400 patients diagnosed with lung cancer are selected from the provincial tumor registry and 400 healthy people are chosen randomly from the population resident in the province. The result of the evaluation of this relationship is OR = 1.67; 95% CI = 1.27 - 2.21. It is true:
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	194	answer	0	There is no relationship between exposure to silica and lung cancer.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	194	answer	1	With this study, the relationship between silica and lung cancer can not be evaluated.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	194	answer	2	It is a randomized experimental study with a positive association between exposure to silica and lung cancer.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	194	answer	3	These data show that there is no statistical significance between exposure to silica and lung cancer.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	194	answer	4	It is a case-control study with a positive association between exposure to silica and lung cancer.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	195	question	-	Regarding the designs of clinical trials to demonstrate efficacy of antidepressants, which of the following is FALSE?
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	195	answer	0	The inclusion in the design of clinical trials of secondary safety variables is important in order to establish the therapeutic location of the drugs studied.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	195	answer	1	In studies of depression it is not considered ethical to use a control group with placebo.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	195	answer	2	The efficacy of antidepressants is measured by the proportion of subjects with a predetermined percentage reduction in specific depression scales, such as Hamilton's or Beck's.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	195	answer	3	Due to the great variability of the response between one study and another, comparisons between active drugs with a noninferiority design do not allow to conclude efficacy in a robust way.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	195	answer	4	Although clinical improvement can be seen in one or two weeks, it usually takes 4 weeks of follow-up in clinical trials to establish significant differences.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	196	question	-	Regarding the phases of the clinical development of a drug, which of the following characteristics does NOT correspond to a clinical trial in phase 2?
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	196	answer	0	They are exploratory therapeutic studies.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	196	answer	1	They are performed in a large number of patients (between 100 and 1000).
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	196	answer	2	They are used to delimit a range of therapeutic doses.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	196	answer	3	The informed consent of the patients is required for their inclusion in the study.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	196	answer	4	They are usually of short duration.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	197	question	-	A randomized phase III clinical trial compared the efficacy of a new analgesic (experimental) with a control treatment (tramadol) in patients with chronic pain. The working hypothesis was that experimental treatment reduces pain more than tramadol. The effect of the two treatments was determined at 48 hours by reducing the score marked by the patient on an analog - visual scale from 0 to 100 mm. The mean reduction in the tramadol group was -27 and in the experimental group of -31. The hypothesis was contrasted for the differences, with the corresponding statistical test and a value of p = 0.03 was obtained. Regarding the previous study, which of the following conclusions seems more correct?
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	197	answer	0	The study demonstrated clinically relevant differences.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	197	answer	1	The differences in the analgesic effect between the two treatments studied were significant.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	197	answer	2	The benefit - risk of the experimental treatment was better than that of tramadol.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	197	answer	3	The experimental treatment was 20% better than tramadol.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	197	answer	4	We can recommend the widespread use of experimental treatment, because it is more effective than tramadol in the treatment of chronic pain.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	198	question	-	A clinical trial is conducted in hypertensive patients to assess the effectiveness of a new drug in reducing the occurrence of heart failure. The Relative Risk (RR) of heart failure in relation to the usual drug is 0.69 with a 95% CI of 0.31 to 1.17. What do these results mean?
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	198	answer	0	The new drug decreases the risk of heart failure significantly.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	198	answer	1	The new drug is very effective and should be marketed.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	198	answer	2	The reduction of the risk of heart failure with the new drug is clinically irrelevant.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	198	answer	3	There are no statistically significant differences between the effect of the drugs studied.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	198	answer	4	The new drug increases the risk of heart failure but not significantly.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	199	question	-	If we define the cut-off point to diagnose renal failure through the Glomerular Filtering Index (GFR) as 15 ml / min instead of 60 ml / min it is increasing:
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	199	answer	0	The sensitivity of the IFG.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	199	answer	1	The specificity of the IFG.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	199	answer	2	The Positive Predictive Value of the IFG.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	199	answer	3	The Negative Predictive Value of the IFG.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	199	answer	4	The internal and external validity of the IFG.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	200	question	-	We have two tests to diagnose a serious prognosis disease. Test A has a sensitivity of 95% and a specificity of 60% and test B has a sensitivity of 70% and a specificity of 99%. We have an effective treatment but that produces important adverse effects and also has a very high cost. What test would we choose to make the diagnosis?
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	200	answer	0	Test A because it will detect less false positives.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	200	answer	1	Test A because it has a higher sensitivity.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	200	answer	2	Test B because it will detect more false positives.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	200	answer	3	Test A because it will detect more false negatives.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	200	answer	4	Test B because it has a greater specificity.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	201	question	-	What type of economic evaluation analysis in health compares the costs of the results of different health interventions measured in years of life adjusted for quality (QALY)?
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	201	answer	0	Analysis of minimization of costs.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	201	answer	1	Cost effectiveness analysis.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	201	answer	2	Cost utility analysis.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	201	answer	3	Cost benefit analysis.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	201	answer	4	Analysis of the cost of health.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	202	question	-	How would you define quality of care?
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	202	answer	0	The quality of care is the set of rules to ensure that things are done well.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	202	answer	1	The quality of care can be defined as the absence of incidents in the care of the sick.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	202	answer	2	The quality of care can be defined as the satisfaction of the needs and aspirations of patients-clients, both real and perceived, with the least consumption of resources.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	202	answer	3	The quality of care is defined through the implementation of improvement actions that ensure compliance with the objectives of the Quality Plan of the Health Center.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	202	answer	4	The quality of care is to treat the sick with all possible means.
Cuaderno_2013_1_M	203	question	-	A 45-year-old patient with a moderate smoking habit, no drinker, no other history of interest who has been diagnosed with squamous carcinoma of the oropharynx cT1N2b. It is pending decision of treatment in committee. What infectious agent would you be interested in confusing before proposing the treatment?
