Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	48	answer	2	600-750 nm.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	48	answer	3	800-2500 nm.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	48	answer	4	3000-8000 nm.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	49	question	-	The voltammetric methods of anodic redissolution:
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	49	answer	0	They are based on the preconcentration of analytes on the surface of an electrode and are characterized by their low sensitivity, since the surface of the electrode is very small.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	49	answer	1	They apply exclusively to the determination of traces of metals by oxidation on a mercury electrode.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	49	answer	2	They require the application of a stage of accumulation and another of redisolution in which intensity-time curves are recorded.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	49	answer	3	They are used for the determination of metals that, once deposited, are oxidized electrochemically.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	49	answer	4	They are based on the variation of the potential that the working electrode experiences as the deposited substance is redissolved.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	50	question	-	The thermal conductivity detector was one of the first used in gas chromatography and still has a lot of application. Consists in:
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	50	answer	0	A source that is heated by electricity, whose temperature at a constant electrical energy depends on the thermal conductivity of the gas that surrounds it.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	50	answer	1	A metallic surface on which ions collide, producing emission of electrons or other ions, with the consequent variation in conductivity.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	50	answer	2	An air / hydrogen flame where organic compounds are pyrolyzed producing ions that lead to a change in conductivity.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	50	answer	3	An emitter of beta radiation that allows the measurement of the conductivity of the eluent that leaves the chromatographic column.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	50	answer	4	A source of thermal ionization that produces positive and negative ions that change the conductivity of the gas passing through the detector.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	51	question	-	The electrolytic cells:
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	51	answer	0	They always have three electrodes.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	51	answer	1	They always have two compartments separated by a salt bridge.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	51	answer	2	They can have a single compartment.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	51	answer	3	They always have a reference electrode.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	51	answer	4	They always use platinum electrodes.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	52	question	-	In some sample treatments, the solid phase extraction technique is used. Said technique:
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	52	answer	0	It has the advantage that solvents are not required.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	52	answer	1	It is applied by contacting the solid sample with the stationary phase contained in a cartridge.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	52	answer	2	It does not require the application of pressure or vacuum.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	52	answer	3	It allows carrying out the preconcentration of large volume samples.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	52	answer	4	It is characterized by the high consumption of solvents.
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	53	question	-	Which of the following transducers are not suitable in Atomic Mass Spectrometry?
Cuaderno_2013_1_Q	53	answer	0	Electron multiplying channels.
