Cuaderno_2015_1_M	86	answer	2	17-OH-progesterone.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	86	answer	3	Dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA).
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	87	question	-	A 35-year-old woman consulted for a right thyroid nodule detected incidentally one morning when a lump was seen in the mirror on the front of the neck. After the relevant studies, it was decided to surgically intervene the patient, performing a total thyroidectomy with emptying of the central lymph node component, the report of the 2.3-cm thyroid nodule pathologist being entirely occupied by a papillary carcinoma of the thyroid, a variant of high cells. no vascular infiltration but capsular. Thyroglobulin concentrations 24 h after thyroidectomy are 14 ng / mL. What would be the next step you would take in this patient?
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	87	answer	0	Send the patient back to the surgeon to perform a right laterocervical lymph node dissection.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	87	answer	1	Start treatment with a dose of TSH-suppressive levothyroxine and review it 6 months later with a new analytical determination of thyroglobulin and a cervical ultrasound.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	87	answer	2	Request a PET-CT scan to see if there is lymph node involvement.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	87	answer	3	Delay the initiation of TSH replacement therapy with levothyroxine and refer the patient to the Nuclear Medicine service for the administration of an ablative dose of 100 mCi of I131.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	88	question	-	The determination in a 70-year-old woman of hematocrit 32%, Hb 9.6 g / dL, VCM 85 fL (7099), iron 25 microg / dL (37-145) and ferritin 350 ng / mL (15-150), It is more suggestive of:
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	88	answer	0	Loss of recent blood through the digestive system.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	88	answer	1	Intestinal malabsorption
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	88	answer	2	Polymyalgia rheumatica.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	88	answer	3	Thalassemia minor.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	89	question	-	Which of the following alterations justifies an extension of the prothrombin time, with normal activated partial thromboplastin time, and that is corrected after incubation with normal plasma ?:
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	89	answer	0	Factor V deficiency
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	89	answer	1	Factor VII deficiency.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	89	answer	2	Factor XII deficiency.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	89	answer	3	Inhibitor acquired against factor VIII.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	90	question	-	A 46-year-old woman with a history of rheumatic valve disease, who requires mitral valve replacement by a mechanical prosthesis. The initial postoperative evolution is favorable. However, after starting treatment with acenocoumarol develops a pattern of skin necrosis affecting the abdominal region and extremities. Which of the following alterations would justify this table?
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	90	answer	0	Antithrombin deficiency.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	90	answer	1	Factor V Leiden.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	90	answer	2	Hyperhomocysteinemia.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	90	answer	3	Protein C deficiency
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	91	question	-	A 51-year-old man admitted from the Emergency Department for macrocytic anemia (Hb 6.3 g / dL, VCM 120 fL). In the studies carried out, a deficiency origin is ruled out. The reticulocytes are 24000 / microL. The bone marrow study is compatible with a myelodysplastic syndrome (MDS). Cytogenetics shows a deletion in 5q. What is the correct statement regarding this patient?
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	91	answer	0	This deletion (5q-) is an alteration of good prognosis and has a specific treatment (lenalidomide).
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	91	answer	1	The recommendation in this patient would be to perform HLA typing to organize an allogeneic transplant.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	91	answer	2	It is a patient with a high International Prognostic Index (IPSS).
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	91	answer	3	The treatment in this case would be transfusions only.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	92	question	-	The hairy cell leukemia (hairy cell leukemia) presents as characteristic clinical-biological features:
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	92	answer	0	Lymphocytosis in peripheral blood greater than 50,000 lymphocytes / microL.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	92	answer	1	T lymphoid line markers
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	92	answer	2	Exfoliative generalized erythroderma, plaques and skin tumors.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	92	answer	3	Pancytopenia and splenomegaly.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	93	question	-	A 25-year-old woman presents to the emergency department for presenting ecchymosis and petechiae in the lower extremities, as well as gingivorrhagia. The hemogram shows the following data: Hb 13 g / dL, leukocytes 8,500 / microL with normal leukocyte formula and platelets 9,000 / mm3. The determination of coagulation times is normal. Which of the following diagnostic tests is NOT required?
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	93	answer	0	JAK-2 mutation.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	93	answer	1	Bone marrow aspirate.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	93	answer	2	Antinuclear antibodies.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	93	answer	3	Peripheral blood smear.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	94	question	-	A 35-year-old patient who, due to ulcerative hematemesis, receives a transfusion of 2 packed red blood cells. 5-10 minutes after the start of the transfusion, the patient begins with fever, chills, hypotension, and pain in the lower back. What would be the most likely diagnosis?
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	94	answer	0	Bacterial contamination of the blood.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	94	answer	1	Febrile reaction secondary to transfusion.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	94	answer	2	Febrile reaction by the plasma that contaminates the red blood cells.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	94	answer	3	Hemolytic transfusion reaction.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	95	question	-	The doctor on call asks the blood bank for blood products for a polytraumatized patient of the A + group. If this were not available, which of the following would be the most appropriate alternative treatment?
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	95	answer	0	Concentrate of red blood cells of group AB + and plasma AB.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	95	answer	1	Concentrate of red blood cells of group B + and plasma AB.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	95	answer	2	Concentrate of red blood cells of group 0+ and plasma AB.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	95	answer	3	Concentrate of red blood cells of group B + and plasma B.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	96	question	-	Regarding graft-versus-host disease (GVHD) post-transplant hemopoietic progenitors. Which of the following statements is false?
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	96	answer	0	The pathophysiological process of GVHD has its origin in the donor T cells.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	96	answer	1	The prophylaxis of GVHD consists of immunosuppressive treatment, frequently cyclosporine associated with methotrexate, and the treatment of established GVHD consists of corticosteroid therapy.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	96	answer	2	The elimination of the lymphocytes from the donor of the graft improves the implant of the parents.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	96	answer	3	In the majority of patients diagnosed chronic GVHD, it disappears and can interrupt the immunosuppressive treatment without the disease recidivising.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	97	question	-	A 64-year-old male farmer, ex-smoker (5 years old), COPD and rheumatoid arthritis in corticosteroid treatment. Consultation in the emergency room due to intense headache of 2 days of evolution with buccal commissure deviation. As an antecedent, he recounts that after a month of flu, cough persists, purulent expectoration and occasionally haemoptica, fever, anorexia, asthenia and weight loss. Upon arrival, there is a fever of 38.2ºC, multiple cutaneous abscesses in the hands, back and buttocks (some with fistulous trajectories) and right central facial paralysis, apical infiltrates with small pleural effusion associated with the chest x-ray and leukocytosis with neutrophilia. . Among the following suspected diagnoses, I would consider MOST probable:
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	97	answer	0	Pulmonary neoplasm with brain metastases.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	97	answer	1	Disseminated tuberculosis.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	97	answer	2	Nocardiosis
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	97	answer	3	Aspergillosis
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	98	question	-	A 52-year-old man who went to the Emergency Department for headache and fever (37.8ºC) of 2 days of evolution. In the last hours it also associates difficulty for the nomination and understanding. In the examination performed, no neck stiffness was observed, the most striking being the presence of a mixed aphasia. The fundus is normal. The cranial CT shows a tenuous hypodensity in the left temporal lobe without mass effect and without contrast uptake. Which of the following statements is correct ?:
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	98	answer	0	Bacterial meningitis is the first diagnostic impression and treatment with 3rd generation cephalosporin should be started as soon as possible.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	98	answer	1	Most likely, the CSF of this patient shows us a predominantly lymphocytic pleocytosis with normal glucose.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	98	answer	2	We would suspect a limbic encephalitis.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	98	answer	3	It is a brain abscess in the initial phase.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	99	question	-	Chikungunya fever is an acute disease transmitted by mosquito bites. It is endemic in certain areas of Africa and Asia. In recent years, cases have been recognized in Europe and America. In relation to it, answer the CORRECT answer:
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	99	answer	0	The characteristic clinical symptom, apart from fever, is debilitating polyarthralgia.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	99	answer	1	The probable geographical origin of the disease is in South Asia.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	99	answer	2	The diagnosis is usually made during the viremic phase by PCR.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	99	answer	3	Mortality is higher in adolescents and young people.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	100	question	-	A 25-year-old man with no relevant medical history came to the emergency department with fever, headache, myalgia, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, jaundice and conjunctival injection, 2 weeks after traveling to Thailand to participate in a freshwater regatta. What is the most likely diagnosis ?:
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	100	answer	0	Malaria.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	100	answer	1	Schistosomiasis
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	100	answer	2	Leptospirosis
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	100	answer	3	Rage.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	101	question	-	A 46-year-old male injecting drug user who presented to the emergency room with fever with chills, mental confusion, diffuse myalgias and intense pain in the left hand of 24 hours of evolution without clear local phlogotic signs. It does not refer any traumatic antecedent. The examination highlights: temperature 38.9ºC, 120 beats per minute, respiratory rate 30 / min, blood pressure 90/54 mm Hg. In the laboratory, a leukocytosis with left deviation stands out (25,000 leukocytes / mm3, 80% neutrophils); increase in creatinine (1.6 mg / dL) and CK (138 U / L). Among the following, what is the most likely diagnosis ?:
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	101	answer	0	Streptococcal necrotizing fasciitis.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	101	answer	1	Gangrene by Clostridium spp.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	101	answer	2	Cellulitis due to mycobacteria.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	101	answer	3	Erysipelas.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	102	question	-	In the presence of a 65-year-old patient with no history of interest who comes to the urgency of a hospital, with fever of 39 ° C, meningeal syndrome and signs of severity (coma, BP 80/60 mmHg, poorly perfused), which of the following actions Do you consider more correct at this time?
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	102	answer	0	Extract blood cultures, perfuse saline and administer treatment with intravenous dexamethasone, ceftriaxone, vancomycin and ampicillin and then perform cranial CT and lumbar puncture if there is no contraindication and reevaluate treatment after CT.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	102	answer	1	CT scan with as much urgency as possible, eye fundus, subsequent CSF culture and antibiotic treatment directed according to the LCR GRAM data.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	102	answer	2	Put IV antipyretic, start treatment with iv dopamine at alpha dose and when hemodynamically stable, do cranial CT, lumbar puncture and initiate guided antibiotic treatment according to the CSF analytical data.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	102	answer	3	Start treatment with intravenous ceftazidime + acyclovir + vancomycin.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	103	question	-	A 32-year-old male from Cameroon consulted for fever cough and pain in the left hemithorax of 1 month of evolution. He took amoxicillin-clavulanate for 1 week without improvement of symptoms. An analysis is carried out with a leukocyte count of 8000 / microL and a hemoglobin of 12.8 g / dL. The chest radiograph shows a loculated left pleural effusion occupying a third of the hemithorax. A thoracentesis shows a yellowish fluid with the following characteristics: red blood cells 2000 / μL, leukocytes 2500 / μL with 90% lymphocytes, proteins 4.9 g / dL, lactate dehydrogenase 550 U / L, glucose 67 mg / dL and absence of cells malignancies in the cytological study. Which of the following tests would be most useful in diagnosing the cause of the pleural effusion?
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	103	answer	0	A thoracic computed tomography (CT).
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	103	answer	1	Measurement of the pH of the pleural fluid.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	103	answer	2	Measurement of adenosine deaminase from pleural fluid.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	103	answer	3	Tuberculin test.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	104	question	-	Patient with a diagnosis of HIV without previous neurological history that is referred to the Emergency Department due to episode of seizure. Which is the LEAST probable etiology?
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	104	answer	0	Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	104	answer	1	Cryptococcal meningitis
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	104	answer	2	HIV encephalopathy
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	104	answer	3	Primary lymphoma of the CNS.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	105	question	-	One of the following drugs is NOT used in the treatment of chronic hepatitis B:
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	105	answer	0	Entecavir.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	105	answer	1	Adefovir.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	105	answer	2	Tenofovir
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	105	answer	3	Sofosbuvir.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	106	question	-	In a patient diagnosed with idiopathic central diabetes insipidus we hope to find (before starting any treatment):
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	106	answer	0	High plasma osmolality, high natremia, increased urine osmolality.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	106	answer	1	Low plasma osmolality, high natremia, increased urinary osmolality.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	106	answer	2	High plasma osmolality, high natremia, decreased urine osmolality.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	106	answer	3	Low plasma osmolality, low natremia, increased urinary osmolality.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	107	question	-	In familial thrombotic microangiopathy due to alteration of the alternative pathway of complement, we would expect to find all the following characteristics, EXCEPT:
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	107	answer	0	Thrombocytosis
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	107	answer	1	Renal insufficiency with microthrombi in glomerular capillaries in renal biopsy.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	107	answer	2	Arterial hypertension.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	107	answer	3	Non-nephrotic hematuria and proteinuria.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	108	question	-	In a patient with rheumatoid arthritis treated with methotrexate, prednisone and indomethacin who presents with acute edema and increased plasma creatinine with a poorly expressed urine sediment and proteinuria less than 100 mg / 24h, the most likely cause is:
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	108	answer	0	Kidney Amyloidosis
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	108	answer	1	Glomerulonephritis secondary to rheumatoid arthritis.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	108	answer	2	Renal insufficiency due to non-steroidal anti-inflammatories.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	108	answer	3	Interstitial nephritis due to methotrexate.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	109	question	-	The MDRD equation allows to calculate the glomerular filtration with an abbreviated formula that includes, in addition to the serum creatinine, the following parameters EXCEPT:
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	109	answer	0	Age.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	109	answer	1	Size.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	109	answer	2	Sex.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	109	answer	3	Race.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	110	question	-	Which of the following manifestations is LESS likely in tubulo-interstitial nephritis ?:
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	110	answer	0	Proteinuria greater than 3.5 g in 24 hours.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	110	answer	1	Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	110	answer	2	Progressive renal failure.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	110	answer	3	Renal tubular acidosis.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	111	question	-	Point out the INCORRECT response to the Hemolytic Uremic Syndrome (HUS):
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	111	answer	0	The condition is characterized by the triad of non-immune microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia and acute renal failure.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	111	answer	1	In some cases, this syndrome is the consequence of a dysregulation of the alternative pathway of the complement system.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	111	answer	2	It is accompanied by hemolytic anemia with positive Coombs test.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	111	answer	3	High levels of lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) and undetectable haptoglobin together with the presence of schistocytes confirm the presence of intravascular hemolysis.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	112	question	-	Regarding the current immunosuppressive treatment used in kidney transplantation, everything is EXCEPT:
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	112	answer	0	Tacrolimus is used less than cyclosporine because it has an increased risk of diabetes.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	112	answer	1	Mycophenolate mofetil inhibits the synthesis of purines and can cause diarrhea.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	112	answer	2	Steroids are adjuvants of other immunosuppressants, block interleukin transcription and are usually used in acute rejection.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	112	answer	3	Sirolimus is usually used in combination with others and may cause hyperlipidemia.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	113	question	-	Which of the following causes of erectile dysfunction reacts with greater sensitivity to intracavernous injection of vasoactive drugs?
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	113	answer	0	Venosa
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	113	answer	1	Arterial.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	113	answer	2	Neurogenic.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	113	answer	3	Hormonal
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	114	question	-	Which of the following groups of drugs used in the treatment of benign prostatic hyperplasia has demonstrated a decrease in the size of the gland, as well as a decreased risk of developing an acute urinary retention?
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	114	answer	0	Inhibitors of 5-alpha reductase (5ARIs).
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	114	answer	1	Inhibitors of phosphodiesterase E5 (PDE5).
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	114	answer	2	Antagonists of alpha adrenergic receptors.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	114	answer	3	Antiangiogenic.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	115	question	-	Within the epidemiology and risk factors in penile carcinoma. What is the FALSE response?
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	115	answer	0	95% of cases of malignant penile tumors are squamous carcinomas.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	115	answer	1	Female couples of male patients with penile cancer do not have a higher incidence of cervical cancer.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	115	answer	2	The involvement of the human papilloma virus (HPV) in its subtypes 16 and 18, increases the risk of penile cancer.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	115	answer	3	Vaccination for boys' HPV is a recommendation in the current calendar.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	116	question	-	A 38-year-old woman, non-smoker, previously diagnosed with atopic rhinitis, presented for a two-month period consisting of cough and intermittent sibilants predominantly nocturnal. Normal chest x-ray. Spirometry within normality with negative bronchodilator test. Which of the following tests would you request next?
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	116	answer	0	Sinus Rx
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	116	answer	1	Thoracic CT.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	116	answer	2	Methacholine test.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	116	answer	3	Prick-test
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	117	question	-	Patient of 46 years of age, hypertensive and without other pathological antecedents of interest. He came to the consultation because he had presented a previous, non-painful left parasternal tumor of two months of evolution and without any other symptom. The physical examination shows a hard mass of 4.5 x 4 cm, not painful, without skin retraction and adhering to deep planes. Which of these statements is correct?
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	117	answer	0	Being a non-painful anterior mass, it should be considered a benign tumor.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	117	answer	1	As a first step, a chondroid tumor should be ruled out since they are the most frequent in this location.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	117	answer	2	It is not indicated to perform a biopsy because of the danger of local dissemination of the tumor.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	117	answer	3	It is preferable to maintain a conservative attitude, evaluating the evolution and possible growth of the tumor.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	118	question	-	Being a medical guard in the Emergency Department of his hospital, he has to attend a 64-year-old patient with acute respiratory failure. His clinical condition is critical, with low oxygen saturation and hemodynamic instability. Urgent chest X-ray is performed showing atelectasis of 2/3 of the right lung. We proceed to orotracheal intubation and ventilation in assisted mode, with FiO2 of 1.0. In an arterial gasometry performed later we obtain these values: pH 7.23; PaO2 60 mmHg and PaCO2 30 mmHg. What is the cause of hypoxemia ?:
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	118	answer	0	Short circuit.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	118	answer	1	Hypoventilation.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	118	answer	2	Low O2 inspired pressure.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	118	answer	3	Neuromuscular disease
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	119	question	-	A 67-year-old man, former smoker, with a diagnosis of severe COPD (multidimensional index BODE 5, FEV1 38%, body mass index 23, dyspnea index according to the mMRC 3 scale, distance traveled in the test of the 6 minutes march 260 m) that has had 3 hospital admissions due to COPD exacerbation in the last 7 months. It also presents a history of hypertension, ischemic heart disease with AMI 5 years ago and intermittent claudication. The clinical examination highlights a decrease in vesicular murmur with expiratory wheeze in both pulmonary fields and a saturation in oximetry of 88%. Which of the following therapeutic strategies would NOT be recommended for this patient ?:
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	119	answer	0	Adjustment of inhaled treatment with long-acting bronchodilators combining anticholinergics and beta-2 adrenergic agents with inhaled glucocorticoids.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	119	answer	1	Initiate oral glucocorticoid regimen for 6 months to control exacerbations.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	119	answer	2	Check that the patient correctly performs the inhalation technique.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	119	answer	3	Initiate chronic home oxygen therapy regimen.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	120	question	-	When gasometry was obtained by breathing ambient air at sea level with pH 7.44, PaO2 55 mmHg and PaCO2 33 mmHg, which of the following diagnoses is LESS likely?
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	120	answer	0	Heart failure.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	120	answer	1	Pneumothorax.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	120	answer	2	Overdose of benzodiazepines.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	120	answer	3	Carbon monoxide poisoning.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	121	question	-	The most frequent cause of chylothorax is:
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	121	answer	0	Mediastinal tumors.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	121	answer	1	Trauma
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	121	answer	2	Hypertriglyceridemia.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	121	answer	3	Lymphoma
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	122	question	-	An ex-smoker patient, previously diagnosed with COPD, undergoes revision. In the physical examination, the existence of digital clubbing of recent appearance is detected. What is the most acceptable explanation for this finding in the clinical context described?
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	122	answer	0	Pulmonary carcinoma
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	122	answer	1	Bronchiectasis
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	122	answer	2	Pulmonary fibrosis.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	122	answer	3	Cyanotic heart disease
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	123	question	-	A 59-year-old woman with a personal history of breast cancer operated on 8 months ago and osteoporosis, suffers a fall and has a fracture of the right femur neck. She is operated on surgically and remains in bed with plaster for 10 days. A week after her high note suddenly a shortness of breath that leads her to consult her GP who notes that her right calf has edema and pain on palpation. What clinical elements would NOT be important in determining that you have a high clinical risk of pulmonary thromboembolism ?:
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	123	answer	0	The history of breast cancer.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	123	answer	1	The history of osteoporosis.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	123	answer	2	The antecedent of having been more than 3 days at rest.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	123	answer	3	The presence of unilateral edema of the right calf.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	124	question	-	55-year-old man who consulted for dysphonia. In the anamnesis, he reported carrying a month with asthenia and unquantified weight loss. The chest radiograph shows an increase in density in the left upper lobe and occupation of the aorto-pulmonary window. The bronchoscopy shows a paralysis of the left vocal cord, without an endoscopic image suggestive of neoplasia. What is the most likely diagnosis ?:
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	124	answer	0	Pulmonary neoplasm
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	124	answer	1	Sarcoidosis
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	124	answer	2	Silicosis.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	124	answer	3	Tuberculosis.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	125	question	-	A 45-year-old patient under study for possible meningitis, with fever, headache and vomiting of 2 days of evolution. They perform a brain MRI and a lumbar puncture. Twenty hours later, when getting up to go to the bathroom, she complains of intense headache, very marked when she gets up but disappears when lying down. No fever or vomiting anymore. He can not walk. Which is most likely the origin of this headache?
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	125	answer	0	Meningitis remains the root cause of your headache, because it is the same type of headache as at the onset of symptoms.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	125	answer	1	It is a lumbar post-puncture headache.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	125	answer	2	A different cause should be sought for this headache, as it is not typical of post-lumbar spinal syndrome or meningitis.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	125	answer	3	Most likely it was not a viral meningitis, but a subarachnoid hemorrhage. For this reason, the initial headache disappears almost completely when lying down.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	126	question	-	A patient diagnosed with multiple sclerosis came to the consultation 6 months ago. What symptomatology would you NOT expect to find?
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	126	answer	0	Diplopia
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	126	answer	1	Auditory alterations.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	126	answer	2	Urinary disorders
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	126	answer	3	Depression.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	127	question	-	A 39-year-old man who in the last 3 months has had two episodes of wide and involuntary saltatory movements of left limbs, which in two minutes lead to loss of consciousness. In the neuroradiological study, a right frontal expansive lesion suggestive of cerebral glioma was found. How would you classify this patient's epilepsy?
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	127	answer	0	Symptomatic generalized.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	127	answer	1	Partial symptomatic.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	127	answer	2	Partial cryptogenic.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	127	answer	3	Partially generalized symptomatic partial.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	128	question	-	A 65-year-old female patient developed speech apraxia two years earlier and has evolved a rigid-akinetic parkinsonism predominantly in the right half of the body with superimposed myoclonus and foreign hand phenomenon or foreign limb. Which diagnosis do you consider most likely?
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	128	answer	0	Parkinson's disease
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	128	answer	1	Corticobasal degeneration.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	128	answer	2	Alzheimer disease.
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	128	answer	3	Huntington's disease
Cuaderno_2015_1_M	129	question	-	A 72-year-old patient came to the clinic with progressive memory loss and difficulty finding the words of a year of evolution. What is the most useful exploration to characterize the type of cognitive deficit that it presents?
