Cuaderno_2015_1_M	226	question	-	An 88-year-old man with autonomy for all activities of daily living and with a history of well controlled hypertension, antiplatelet atrial fibrillation and a prostate adenocarcinoma at 78 years of age, currently free of disease. He is brought to the emergency room for acute aphasia and right hemiparesis, 45 minutes before. What is the most correct attitude?
