Cuaderno_2016_1_M	29	answer	2	Intradermal reaction with autologous serum.
Cuaderno_2016_1_M	29	answer	3	None, anamnesis and clinical exploration is enough.
Cuaderno_2016_1_M	30	question	-	Question linked to image no.31 A 26-year-old woman consulted for a fever of 3 weeks evolution. The fever was daily, in peaks that reached 38.5 ° C and sometimes at 39 ° C, and was accompanied by pharyngeal pain, myalgias and arthralgias of large joints. Within a few hours the fever subsided spontaneously or by some antipyretic. During the febrile episodes it presented a rash. The patient reported that she had presented two previous episodes in the previous two years, very similar to the current one, which had not been diagnosed despite in-depth studies. He had not made trips outside of Spain nor did he refer epidemiological antecedents of interest. The clinical examination during a febrile episode showed the skin rash that can be seen in the image, which disappeared in a few hours. The patient also had pharyngeal hyperemia, bilateral small painful laterocervical lymphadenopathies and a spleen pole was palpated below the left costal arch. To establish the diagnosis, it is expected that several laboratory and imaging studies should be performed. However, which of the following statements seems more appropriate in the current diagnostic phase?
Cuaderno_2016_1_M	30	answer	0	It would request serological tests for hepatitis B and C, human parvovirus B19, Epstein-Barr virus and test for the human immunodeficiency virus, since the most likely cause are infectious diseases, especially acute viral infections.
Cuaderno_2016_1_M	30	answer	1	Although it is a diagnosis of exclusion, I would request a serum ferritin because of the possibility that it is an adult Still's disease.
Cuaderno_2016_1_M	30	answer	2	It would request an immunological study that included antinuclear antibodies, rheumatoid factor, citrullinated cyclic antiseptide antibodies and ANCAs to confirm the suspicion of a systemic rheumatic disease such as rheumatoid arthritis or systemic lupus erythematosus.
Cuaderno_2016_1_M	30	answer	3	I would request a genetic study given that it is most likely a familial Mediterranean fever type autoinflammatory disease.
Cuaderno_2016_1_M	31	question	-	Question linked to image no. 32 The hands shown in the image belong to a 52-year-old woman who consulted because she presented 3 years of intermittent mild intermittent pain in the distal and proximal interphalangeal joints of both hands. The pain is aggravated after intense efforts and may be accompanied by swelling and redness. He has taken non-steroidal anti-inflammatories during these periods, with improvement. After deforming, the joints bother you less. Which diagnosis is the most likely?
