Cuaderno_2016_1_M	222	question	-	A 36-year-old man was referred to the Surgery office after urgent drainage of perianal abscess two months before, persisting suppuration. The patient was being previously studied for digestive by diarrhea, abdominal pain and weight loss. Colonoscopy: Rectum of normal appearance; at the level of the descending colon and sigma, alternating areas with a "cobbled" appearance and linear and deep ulcers alternate with normal-looking ones. Endoanal echo and MRI were completed with a high transsphincter fistula. Without current medical treatment. What would be the most appropriate initial treatment of the fistula?
