Cuaderno_2017_1_Q	0	question	-	In atomic emission spectroscopy with induction-coupled plasma, it is usually used as a plasmogenic gas:
Cuaderno_2017_1_Q	0	answer	0	Helium because it forms excited species in a metastable state capable of exciting elements with high energies such as halogens and non-metals.
Cuaderno_2017_1_Q	0	answer	1	Argon because it is chemically inert, monatomic, has a high ionization energy capable of exciting most elements of the periodic system and its emission spectrum is simple.
Cuaderno_2017_1_Q	0	answer	2	Oxygen because it is an affordable and cheap, diatomic gas and during atomization molecular species are formed, which increases the emission signal.
Cuaderno_2017_1_Q	0	answer	3	Hydrogen, because ions and electrons are produced during the atomization and compounds with functional groups such as carbonyl, alcohol, halogen or amine produce few ions or practically none in the plasma.
Cuaderno_2017_1_Q	1	question	-	How can the limit molar conductivity of a strong electrolyte be calculated ?:
Cuaderno_2017_1_Q	1	answer	0	Reading it directly on the screen of a conductimeter calibrated in units of Siemen / cm.
Cuaderno_2017_1_Q	1	answer	1	By extrapolation of the adjustment line derived from the Ostwald dilution law.
